  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing clouds today especially across northern MA. \nHighs in the middle 70s-lower 80s. A few brief light showers possible \ntoward evening especially across western and northern MA.\n\nLarge high pressure moves off the coast today as a cold front and \nshortwave energy approach from the west. This will result in \nincreasing southwest flow today and a gradual increase in humidity. \nWe also do expect to see an increase in clouds in the warm advection\npattern and developing southwest LLJ as the day wears along. In \nfact... we may see a few brief light showers toward evening \nespecially across western and northern MA. High temperatures will \nmainly be in the middle 70s to the lower 80s. A southwest breeze \nwill also kick in during the afternoon with gusts on the order of 15 \nto 25 mph. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Gusty SW winds tonight into Thu and becoming humid \nas well. Brief round of showers tonight into early Thu \nespecially in northern MA. Unseasonably warm Thu...Highs middle to \nupper 80s.\n\nAs the actual cold front approaches from the west tonight into early \nThu the southwest LLJ will increase further. All the guidance is in \nfairly good agreement in a southwest LLJ peaking between 45 and 55 \nknots over this time. Bufkit soundings also indicate some modest \nmixing too. This should yield southwest wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph \nwith a few brief gusts near 35 mph in the Worcester Hills and near \nthe I-95 corridor. However...the strongest winds will likely be \ntowards the Cape/Islands particularly in the climatologically \nfavored spots with this setup near Buzzards Bay/Upper Cape. GFS/NAM \nsoundings near KFMH indicating brief 35-45 mph wind gusts and not \nout of the question a few spots briefly flirt with Wind Advisory \ncriteria. \n\nLastly...the other issue will be potential for some \nshowers. Pwats are quite impressive on the order of 2+ inches \ntonight into early Thu. However...the better dynamics will be to our \nnorth and the soundings indicate some mid level dry air which will \nbe limiting factors. Nonetheless...we still expect a brief round of \nshowers tonight into early Thu especially across northern \nMA. However...they will not last long at a given location. In \nfact...plenty of sunshine should develop on Thu and 925T on the \norder of +20C to +22C should yield highs in the middle to upper 80s \nin many locations. Westerly wind gusts of 20-30 mph are also \nexpected with a bit of humidity too...especially during the first \nhalf of the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry and very pleasant weather follows Fri into Sat. \nHighs in the  70s with low humidity. Quite cool Fri night/Sat am \nwith lows in the 40s to the lower 50s and perhaps even some upper \n30s in the normally coolest low lying locations. \n\nPost frontal airmass will result in beautiful weather Fri into Sat \nwith low humidity. Plenty of sunshine expected both days with highs \nin the 70s along with very comfortable humidity. High pressure \noverhead will allow for lows in the the 40s to the lower 50s with \neven some upper 30s possible in the normally coolest low-lying \nlocations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Above normal temps return for the first half of next \nweek with a brief round of scattered showers possible late Sat night-\nSun am.\n\nHigh pressure moves off the coast for the second half of the weekend \nas a warm front crosses the region. This may bring a brief period of \nscattered showers to the region late Sat night into Sun morning. Its \nmain impact will be a southwest flow of warmer air working back into \nthe region. Ensembles are in agreement in above normal height fields \nand 2 meter temp anomalies for the first half of next week. This \nsupports above normal temps for the first half of next week...mainly \nin the upper 70s to the middle 80s.",
